[00:03.14]Proposition forty-four sets a spending rule for private nonprofit
[00:06.96]safety-net clinics.
[00:08.86]These clinics serve many low-income and uninsured patients.
[00:12.30]The proposal does not apply the same rule to
[00:14.36]every doctor, hospital, or publicly operated clinic.
[00:18.54]Covered clinics would have to spend at least ninety
[00:21.12]percent
[00:21.98]of annual revenue on health care services,
[00:24.96]leaving no more than ten percent for other expenses.
[00:28.00]The attorney general would define the categories in more
[00:30.68]detail.
[00:31.70]Imagine a clinic manager planning next year’s budget.
[00:34.32]For a hypothetical clinic receiving ten million dollars, the
[00:38.24]ninety percent minimum is nine million dollars.
[00:41.64]If qualifying spending is eight million, the shortfall is
[00:45.52]one million.
[00:47.08]This is arithmetic to explain the rule, not a
[00:49.76]real clinic’s budget.
[00:51.14]A nurse depends on both care resources and working
[00:54.50]systems.
[00:55.96]A clinic falling short would owe a penalty equal
[00:58.94]to the shortfall.
[01:00.16]It could recover that money by complying within five
[01:02.62]years.
[01:03.74]Temporary waivers would be available in some circumstances.
[01:07.62]The rule therefore has enforcement mechanisms as well as
[01:10.90]a target.
[01:12.10]Which costs qualify matters.
[01:14.46]An administrative label is not proof that an expense
[01:16.91]is wasteful;
[01:18.49]scheduling, records, and other systems can support care.
[01:22.33]Final definitions affect how clinics would change their budgets.
[01:25.73]The analyst estimates enforcement costs in the low tens
[01:28.59]of millions of dollars each year, funded by fees
[01:31.67]on affected clinics.
[01:34.21]Other public costs are uncertain and depend on how
[01:36.85]clinics respond,
[01:38.39]including whether some reduce operations or close.
[01:42.11]A voter might want a larger share of clinic
[01:44.37]revenue devoted to services for patients, with reporting and
[01:48.01]consequences for falling short.
[01:50.45]They may believe a clear minimum is a useful
[01:53.01]accountability tool.
[01:55.33]Reaching a ratio, however, does not by itself measure
[01:59.09]quality or outcomes.
[02:01.25]You might ask whether one ratio fits different clinics.
[02:04.93]Another voter could worry that a rigid limit constrains
[02:07.99]necessary support costs,
[02:09.93]adds fees, or makes some clinics financially fragile.
[02:14.09]A clinic can improve its reported ratio in different
[02:17.01]ways;
[02:18.51]the important outcome is access to effective care, not
[02:21.55]only the percentage on a report.
[02:24.27]In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 34
[02:27.17]percent said yes, 61 percent no, and 6 percent
[02:30.81]were unsure.
